### Differentiating using the power rule

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{^{\Large\frac{2}{5}}}\right)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{5}x^{^{\Large\frac{2}{5}-1}}\qquad\gray{\text{The power rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac25x^{^{\Large-\frac{3}{5}}}
\end{align}$

### Evaluating the derivative

So we found that $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\sqrt[5]{x^2}\right)=\dfrac25x^{^{\Large-\frac{3}{5}}}$, which can also be written as $\dfrac{2}{5(\sqrt[5]{x})^3}$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=32}$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{2}{5\left(\sqrt[5]{\blueD{32}}\right)^3}&=\dfrac{2}{5(2)^3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{5\cdot 8}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{20}
\end{align}$
